        "One August afternoon, twelve high school students set out from their school gate with clipboards, cheap digital thermometers and a printed map of the center of their city. Every thirty minutes they stopped at the same twenty points — a bus stop, a parking lot, a narrow lane lined with old wooden houses, a wide avenue planted with zelkova trees — and wrote down the temperature of the air and of the ground. Their science teacher, Ms. Okada, had given them a simple question: is every part of the city equally hot? By the end of the day the answer was clear. The parking lot measured 38°C in the air and more than 55°C on the asphalt, while the avenue under the trees, only three hundred meters away, was almost six degrees cooler. “{u:(1):The city has a fever,}” one student wrote in her notebook, “but not everywhere.”",
        "Scientists call this pattern the urban heat island effect. Concrete, asphalt and roofs soak up sunlight during the day and give the heat back slowly at night, {b:ア} cars, air conditioners and factories add heat of their own. As a result, the center of a large city can be several degrees warmer than the countryside around it, especially after sunset. This is not just a matter of comfort. During heat waves, hospitals receive more patients suffering from heatstroke, and the danger is greatest for elderly people who live alone in rooms that never cool down at night.",
        "Trees are one of the oldest and cheapest tools for cooling a city. Their most obvious gift is shade: a large tree can block most of the sunlight that would otherwise heat the ground and the walls of buildings. Less obvious, but just as important, is what happens inside the leaves. Trees pull water up from the soil and release it into the air as vapor, a process that uses energy and cools the air around them, much as sweat cools our skin. Trees along a street also slow the wind a little and catch some of the dust thrown up by traffic. {b:イ}, people simply prefer to walk on shaded streets, which means fewer short trips by car on hot days.",
        "If trees are so useful, why do many cities have so few of them? {u:(4):The reasons are surprisingly practical.} A tree needs room for its roots, but under most sidewalks there are water pipes, gas lines and electric cables. Fallen leaves block drains and must be swept up every autumn, and shop owners sometimes complain that branches hide their signs. Young trees need regular watering for their first few years, and in many cities the money for this work is the first to be cut. {b:ウ}, a tree planted today will not give much shade for ten or twenty years — longer than most mayors stay in office.",
